1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Welcome. In this lab you will run the delivered TRACE Small Flood-SAR teaching simulator and finish a controller that turns a TRACE consumer action plus visible resource state into an allocation or a refusal.

2
00:00:40,000 --> 00:01:40,000
The goal is one complete, understandable rescue path, not a reimplementation of the full research simulator. Small is synthetic and reduced-order, and student variants are not research results.

3
00:01:40,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Your code begins at the downstream rescue-controller boundary. It checks whether TRACE permits use and whether compatible, reachable, available capacity exists.

4
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Preflight performs no network request and does not write scientific artifacts. Run it before the room starts coding.

5
00:03:55,000 --> 00:05:00,000
The fresh deterministic scenario produces eight allocations, twelve refusals, and eight visible-evidence repairs. Replay regenerates the run and compares every byte.

6
00:05:00,000 --> 00:06:30,000
The capacity-refusal case is crucial: TRACE clears, but no compatible resource is currently available. Authorization and allocation are different layers.

7
00:06:30,000 --> 00:07:45,000
The resource ID tie-break prevents input order from changing dispatch when travel times tie.

8
00:07:45,000 --> 00:09:15,000
A consumer action other than CLEAR must refuse before dispatch. With CLEAR, call your resource filter. No eligible resource produces a capacity refusal; otherwise allocate the first deterministic resource.

9
00:09:15,000 --> 00:10:30,000
Append-only history lets a reviewer see what the controller represented and why it acted at each time.

10
00:10:30,000 --> 00:11:30,000
Your decisions should match the public walkthrough. The scaffold owns artifact loading and contract validation; your code owns downstream controller choices.

11
00:11:30,000 --> 00:12:45,000
The variants alter copied lab resource views only. They show the layer boundary without changing TRACE, the canonical book, or a registered result.

12
00:12:45,000 --> 00:13:45,000
The retained evidence follows original and recovery artifact-retention failures. Describe that history exactly.

13
00:13:45,000 --> 00:14:50,000
The audit chain explains what the controller represented and why it acted. It does not prove real-world truth, optimality, or operational readiness.
